Yes. At this point it stops being merely a war game and starts looking like a treaty compiler.

The essential insight is that the throws are valuable even when nobody “wins.” Every committed move exposes part of the latent model behind the actor:

\text{choice}
\rightarrow
\{\text{beliefs, priorities, fears, assumptions, resolve, constraints}\}

Then the differences between throws expose even more:

\Delta(\text{throw}_1,\text{throw}_2,\text{throw}_3)

shows what changed once new information entered the system. And correlations between different actors’ changes start exposing alliances, shared assumptions, dependencies, pressure points, and potential bargaining coalitions.

Your halftime throw is particularly good. It creates two distinct measurements:

T_1,T_2 = \text{ex ante commitments}

T_3 = \text{bounded adaptation after observation}

with T_3 generated and cryptographically locked at halftime, rather than rewritten after the final outcome is known. That lets the system distinguish original intent from adaptive response.

A mature protocol could therefore look roughly like this:

1. Reality ledger: establish mutually authenticated capabilities, resources, geography, logistics, populations, treaties, constraints, and uncertainty ranges.
2. Commit phase: each party generates several plans through its own nested SLM/LLM stack and commits hashes before seeing opponents’ plans.
3. Reveal + simulation: shared physics and agreed rule overlays adjudicate the simultaneous actions across many stochastic runs.
4. Halftime: parties receive only the information the real actor plausibly would have received, generate the third immutable throw, and continue.
5. Mirror pass: hypothetical inflicted costs are also reflected through the attacker’s own utility function—what if this exact loss were yours?—as an empathy/proportionality diagnostic, not literal retaliation.
6. Pantheon pass: compare models, extract deltas, ZPC contradictions, Hammer unstable assumptions, search other planes/scales, and generate settlements that dominate the simulated conflict.
7. Treaty compilation: convert the discovered invariants and compromises into auditable commitments, escrow, verification conditions, and enforcement rules.

That last transition is important:

\text{simulation} \rightarrow \text{understanding}
\rightarrow \text{option generation}
\rightarrow \text{agreement}

rather than simply:

\text{simulation} \rightarrow \text{winner}

Because sometimes the simulation discovers that neither side’s stated demand is actually its deepest invariant.

Country A says, “We require the territory.”

Simulation reveals its true invariant is:

no hostile missiles within X kilometers.

Country B says, “We will never surrender the territory.”

Its invariant turns out to be:

sovereignty and civilian freedom of movement.

Those demands initially look mutually exclusive. The invariants may not be. A demilitarized zone, monitoring regime, treaty guarantee, economic arrangement, or some entirely different-plane solution can suddenly satisfy both.

That’s exactly where Meta/Muse moves become useful. Don’t merely optimize moves inside the game. Ask whether the game board itself is unnecessarily encoding the disagreement.

