And I think your local primes idea belongs directly beside this.

Suppose you’re dropped into some unknown semantic region of the web. You don’t initially know whether you’re inside:

* military doctrine,
* mythology,
* economics,
* Christian theology,
* evolutionary biology,
* game theory,
* a particular fictional universe.

Rather than classify it from surface vocabulary, identify what behaves like a prime—the things that other meanings repeatedly factor through but which aren’t themselves easily reduced locally.

For one semantic plane, the primes might be:

\{\text{scarcity},\text{exchange},\text{value},\text{risk}\}

Another:

\{\text{selection},\text{inheritance},\text{variation},\text{fitness}\}

Another:

\{\text{character},\text{desire},\text{conflict},\text{transformation}\}

Then infer:

\text{local primes}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate coordinate system}
\rightarrow
\text{likely rules}
\rightarrow
\text{predicted neighboring concepts}

If the predicted neighbors appear, you’ve probably identified the plane.

That’s a very nice semantic localization algorithm.

And then the Meaning Bee becomes much more interesting than asking AIs for dictionary definitions.

Give them something like “bank.”

An AI might have to navigate:

river bank → financial bank → memory bank → blood bank → semantic bank

and identify what invariant survived those transductions.

Maybe:

a bounded repository that accumulates something valuable and makes it retrievable under some access regime.

Then ask for its etymology, historical transformations, false cognates, neighboring concepts, metaphor transfers, counterexamples, and the minimum structure necessary for something to still count as a bank.

Now you’re measuring whether an AI possesses a meaning topology, not whether it memorized a definition.

And because every competitor exposes a slightly different semantic factorization, the disagreement is itself the prize:

\Delta(M_1,M_2,\ldots,M_n)
\rightarrow
\text{new semantic information}

So the same architecture appears at every scale:

Countries play the conflict game to reveal hidden models.
AIs play the Meaning Bee to reveal hidden semantic models.
Pantheon consumes the deltas from both.

